    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="2" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="false" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="false">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="17.5" heatingNighttime="19.5" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="0" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, the air leakage from your home is reasonable, however there are a few key areas that should be addressed. The door latch &amp; lock on the front entrance door (from the cold porch into the house) are broken &amp; missing, so this door does not close tightly. The right side entrance door had some plastic up when I was there however the door did seem to seal pretty well when I conducted the blower door depressurization test. Overall - both doors are reasonable and can likely just be maintained by ensuring the door locks/latches + weatherstripping are all installed &amp; in good working order.



One other key air leakage area is the 8" open combustion air pipe that brings in outside air for the furnace. If the furnace is upgraded to a higher efficiency "direct vent" model, this 8" open pipe can be eliminated.



Some other smaller air leakage in your house is from around &amp; through many of the electrical outlets &amp; wall switches located on exterior walls. For these electrical outlets, you can install the specially designed foam gaskets behind the cover plates.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 135&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <windows>

For your longer term energy efficiency planning, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of the original double pane slider windows to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the older double pane windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 196&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. As discussed, the payback on windows (from strictly an energy savings perspective) is quite long, however most homeowners consider upgrading for other reasons - current windows have broken hardware or frames, the seals between the glass have deteriorated, wanting to switch from sliders to casement, etc. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank, and the cost of electricity in Tuktoyaktuk is very expensive. In fact, electricity in Tuktoyaktuk is generated using diesel and the efficiency of the diesel generators is often less than if you used the oil directly to create the hot water. I did see in this house that it used to have an oil hot water heater (that was co-vented up the same chimney as the furnace), however it was replaced with an electric hot water tank



The Arctic Energy Alliance is starting to see a variety of higher efficiency oil hot water heaters being installed in all regions of the Northwest Territories (most are direct vent through a side wall, rather than up a chimney). Although we do not recommend or endorse any manufacturers or products, I will mention a unit that we are seeing more NWT contractors starting to install - the Toyotomi OM-122HW. We are advised by Natural Resources Canada that this unit is not currently Canadian ENERGY STARÂ® rated, however they indicate it has an Energy Factor of 0.682.



The AEA adjudicates the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP) and the current guidelines indicate that an instantaneous on-demand oil hot water heater must have a minimum 0.68 EF and be Canadian ENERGY STARÂ® rated. As such, the Toyotomi OM-122HW is not eligible for the $1,250 EEIP rebate ........... however we are currently reviewing this with the GNWT - so please call me or check our website for an update. 





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot electric hot water tank with an instantaneous oil-fired water heater with a 0.682 EF, there would be a small increase in overall energy use, however the cost difference between oil &amp; electricity indicates that you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save up to $ 999&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. As mentioned above, this upgrade does not currently qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace appears to be the original unit and is rated at 82.1% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), and it vents up through a chimney. There is an 8" open combustion air pipe that brings in combustion air for the furnace. That 8" pipe was originally sized for both the furnace and hot water tank, however the hot water tank has since been converted from oil to electric. The furnace also has a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the back to bring in fresh air to the house (whenever an exhaust fan is operating), and the furnace fan then evenly distributes the fresh air throughout the house.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified, direct vent oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This type of furnace would eliminate the need for the 8" open combustion air pipe.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current oil furnace with a higher-efficiency 87% AFUE direct vent model (eliminating the need for the 8" open combustion air pipe), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 276&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. To qualify for the &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, oil furnaces need to be a minimum 92% AFUE ........... however I do not currently see any mechanical contractors in your region installing this type of furnace. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has a toilet that is an older model, and you should consider replacing it with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



This house has a central ventilation design that includes the following:



1. A Continental "inline" exhaust fan in the lower heated crawl (rated at 190 CFM) that has the following connected:

     - The bathroom fan - wall switch operates the inline fan

     - The kitchen fan - wall switch operates the inline fan

     - The hallway fan - wall humidistat switch operates the inline fan

     - There is a "summer switch" located beside the furnace - that operates the inline fan



2. Whenever the inline fan is operated, the furnace fan is designed to come on &amp; draw in outside fresh air (from the 5" pipe that feeds into the back on the furnace) to replace the air being exhausted &amp; distribute the incoming fresh air evenly throughout the house.



3. I also noticed that the range hood fan in the kitchen is piped into this inline fan &amp; I have never seen this before. Kitchen range hood fans are normally vented directly down &amp; out through the bottom floor. You may want to investigate this further, as having the range hood fan connected into the ductwork feeding into the inline fan may be a code violation ?



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, the Continental inline fan and the dryer, indicated a pressure drop of 2.7 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products (from the current furnace) to be drawn into your home.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke detectors, however the carbon monoxide (CO) detector was missing. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date (normally every 10 years).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Tuktoyktuk&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2017 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Tuktoyaktuk:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Oil  - $1.35 per litre 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Ken Baigent

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
